  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Evaluating, interpreting and transforming functions runs through the Advanced Math domain.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "thm",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Reference — Functions on the SAT",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Evaluate \\( f(a) \\) by substitution and read features (intercepts, vertex) from graphs. \\( f(x)+k \\) shifts the graph vertically; \\( f(x-h) \\) shifts it horizontally.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "figure",
        "figure": {
          "type": "fn",
          "title": "Function Transformations: Horizontal & Vertical Shifts",
          "xrange": [-3, 7],
          "yrange": [-1, 9],
          "fns": [
            { "fn": "x^2", "color": "var(--fig-indigo-2)", "label": "Parent: f(x) = x^2 (vertex at 0,0)" },
            { "fn": "(x - 3)^2 + 2", "color": "var(--fig-amber)", "label": "Transformed: g(x) = (x - 3)^2 + 2" }
          ],
          "points": [
            { "x": 0, "y": 0, "label": "(0, 0)" },
            { "x": 3, "y": 2, "label": "Vertex (3, 2)" }
          ]
        },
        "caption": {
          "en": "The graph of $g(x) = (x - 3)^2 + 2$ is obtained by shifting the parent parabola $f(x) = x^2$ right by 3 units and up by 2 units, placing its vertex at $(3, 2)$.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Evaluate",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(a) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Composite",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(g(x)) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Shift up",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(x)+k \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Shift right",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( f(x-h) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 8.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>If \\( f(x)=x^{2}-1 \\), find \\( f(4) \\).</p>\\[ 16-1=15. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "▲ Piecewise &amp; Absolute Value Functions",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "A <strong>piecewise function</strong> uses a different rule on each part of its domain. To evaluate, find which condition \\(x\\) satisfies, then apply only that piece. The <strong>absolute value function</strong> is itself piecewise: \\(f(x)=|x|=x\\) when \\(x\\geq0\\) and \\(-x\\) when \\(x&lt;0\\). Its graph is a V-shape with vertex where the expression equals zero.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Piecewise eval",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Match \\(x\\) to the right interval",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "\\(|ax+b|\\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "V-shape; vertex at \\(x=-b/a\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Continuity",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Do pieces meet at boundary?",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "\\(|f(x)|=k\\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\(f(x)=k\\) or \\(f(x)=-k\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 8.B — Piecewise function",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>\\(f(x)=\\begin{cases}x^{2} &amp; x&lt;2 \\\\ 3x-1 &amp; x\\geq2\\end{cases}\\). Find \\(f(-1)\\) and \\(f(4)\\).</p><p>\\(f(-1)\\): use \\(x^2\\). \\((-1)^2=\\mathbf{1}\\).</p><p>\\(f(4)\\): use \\(3x-1\\). \\(3(4)-1=\\mathbf{11}\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "explorer",
        "title": {
          "en": "⊙ Explorer · Evaluating a piecewise function",
          "ar": null
        },
        "canvasId": "satPieceCanvas",
        "ariaLabel": {
          "en": "A piecewise function graph, x squared on the left piece and 3x minus 1 on the right piece, with a movable evaluation point",
          "ar": null
        },
        "dataPanel": {
          "btnId": "satPieceDataBtn",
          "panelId": "satPieceDataPanel",
          "descId": "satPieceDataDesc",
          "rowsId": "satPieceDataRows",
          "caption": {
            "en": "f(x) sampled across both pieces",
            "ar": null
          },
          "columns": [
            { "en": "x", "ar": null },
            { "en": "f(x)", "ar": null },
            { "en": "active piece", "ar": null }
          ]
        },
        "controls": [
          {
            "id": "satPieceX",
            "label": { "en": "evaluate at <b>x</b>:", "ar": null },
            "min": -4, "max": 6, "value": -1, "step": 0.5,
            "valueDisplayId": "satPieceXval",
            "ariaLabel": { "en": "x-value to evaluate", "ar": null }
          }
        ],
        "readouts": [
          {
            "id": "satPiecePiece",
            "label": { "en": "active piece", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "amber",
            "value": "x²"
          },
          {
            "id": "satPieceF",
            "label": { "en": "f(x)", "ar": null },
            "colorClass": "indigo",
            "value": "1.000"
          }
        ],
        "note": {
          "en": "This is exactly Worked example 8.B's f(x): x² for x &lt; 2 (indigo), 3x − 1 for x ≥ 2 (amber). Drag x to −1 or 4 to reproduce the worked example's f(−1) = 1 and f(4) = 11, then drag across x = 2 to see the two pieces don't actually meet there.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠️ SAT Traps & Pitfalls — Function Transformations & Notation",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<ul><li><strong>Horizontal Shift Direction:</strong> $f(x - c)$ shifts the graph $c$ units to the <em>right</em>, whereas $f(x + c)$ shifts it $c$ units to the <em>left</em>.</li><li><strong>Input vs. Output:</strong> For $f(x) = 12$, you are finding the input $x$ that yields 12 (solving $f(x) = 12$), NOT calculating $f(12)$!</li><li><strong>Composition Evaluation Order:</strong> In $f(g(x))$, evaluate the innermost expression $g(x)$ first, then pass the resulting value into $f$.</li><li><strong>Undefined Points / Domain Restrictions:</strong> Radical functions require non-negative radicands ($x \\ge 0$), and rational functions are undefined where the denominator is zero.</li></ul>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Exam Practice 8.C — Function Composition & Domain",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Question:</strong> For the functions $f(x) = 3x - 5$ and $g(x) = x^2 + 2$, what is the value of $g(f(2))$?</p><p><strong>Solution:</strong></p><ol><li>Evaluate the inner function first: $f(2) = 3(2) - 5 = 6 - 5 = 1$.</li><li>Substitute this result into the outer function: $g(1) = (1)^2 + 2 = 1 + 2 = 3$.</li></ol><p>Thus, $g(f(2)) = 3$.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "<strong>SAT-style:</strong> \\(g(x)=\\begin{cases}2x+5&amp;x\\leq-1 \\\\ x^{2}-3&amp;x&gt;-1\\end{cases}\\). What is \\(g(-1)+g(3)\\)?",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "\\(g(-1)=2(-1)+5=3\\). \\(g(3)=9-3=6\\). Sum \\(=\\mathbf{9}\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "quizWidget": {
    "domains": ["Functions & Function Notation"],
    "enabled": true
  }
}
